00:25:56.968 --> 00:32:53.048
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. So like I said, the property was in disrepair and Mr. Bigelow, we know very little about what Mr. Bigelow did or did not do during his time on Skinwalker Ranch. It's still locked away behind, it's still hidden and he hasn't come forth with any information. In turning over the ranch to Brandon, the only thing that Brandon received when he purchased the ranch was the real estate documents that are required by law, you know, soils tests and some of the things that prove that the property is, there's nothing dangerous on the property as far as radioactive or in Utah, real estate disclosures require that you disclose, you know, if there's something wrong with the property, you're supposed to disclose it to the buyer. So there was those type of things. But there was no scientific data that was turned over, nothing. And when we came on, it was hard to tell that there had been a scientific effort at all. And so we basically started from scratch. Brandon's, that first meeting that I was with Brandon, he explained to me that he bought this property, and he wasn't even sure if there was anything to all of this. Because Mr. Fugal hadn't transferred over any data, he wasn't sure if this was a bunch of stories or if there was actually something strange happening at the ranch. And so he wanted me to meet his principal investigator, Eric Bard, who's a trusted confidant of Brandon's. And one of the greatest scientists, I mean, this guy is just brilliant and so disciplined in his approach and so meticulous about the way that he does his science. He's above reproach. And Brandon said, for the first little while, I want you to make as little disturbances as possible. And I just want to observe and see if we can see anything going on. And so even when we went and put cameras up, Bigelow had some old camera. He'd put his cameras inside of these metal housing casings out there on a pole. And so we actually just took his cameras out because they were obsolete and just they were no good. And when we put our cameras, we actually put them inside just so that the outside appearance didn't change at all. We didn't want to broadcast any that we were there, really, that we were trying to watch. And so we were just in a state of observation. And so for the first 12 to even maybe 16 months that Brandon owned the ranch, we were in an observation period where we were just trying to see, is there something happening at Skinwalker Ranch? And it didn't take very long. And matter of fact, Brandon had his first UFO sighting on the ranch, I think six months after he purchased the property, you know, going into the fall here. But it didn't take very long before we started to witness things that were unexplainable. And the answer became very clear that, yes, there is something strange taking place on Skinwalker Ranch. And then, about, I'd say early 2017, the discussion began to shift from, is there something happening to, there is something happening now, what do we do about it? What is the next step? And that's when we started to transition into taking some pretty aggressive steps to update the infrastructure. Brandon is very wealthy, but he's wealthy because he's smart with his money. He just didn't start dumping money a million directions. Our investment has always been, it's always followed the data. So for example, the first camera system that we put out on the ranch was a very inexpensive, low grade, low quality system. It was simply there to just see if we could capture. And once we started capturing stuff, then Brandon incrementally started to increase the quality of the cameras and the surveillance systems that we had based on the data that we were getting. And we've spent money on things and haven't got any results. And so there's no sense spending even more money in that area. And so our spending has been focused solely on where we're getting the results. So my time out at the ranch in the last four years was, it is a ranch that requires care. It needs to be irrigated. You have to get livestock on there that turns into a big fire hazard because the grass grows two feet tall in the spring. And then because we're in a desert by August, it's just so dead and dry that even a spark would just burn the whole place down. The cows come in, they eat the grass down and then it doesn't do that. So the fences, I mean, there's so much maintenance just with the regular ranch that takes place that I was trying to take care of. And then when you add on to that, the science, once 2017 started, that's when we started to make aggressive changes. And really, so then the whole narrative of my injury comes into play because it was, it was March of 2017 when I had that severe injury. And it may be coincidentally, maybe not. We don't know for sure. We can't say positively. But, that's when I started to do some major excavation on the ranch in conjunction with upgrading the facilities. And then, you know, I start, I start changing the landscape drastically, putting in some roads and things for security to be able to get around on the ranch. And then I end up with this injury. So I've been very busy over the past four years. I've installed every single camera on the place. Every, I've run miles and miles of power lines and internet lines. I've upgraded the septic, the septic system, the wastewater system was completely shot. We've replaced that. The old ranch house had a lot of mold issues and stuff in it that we had to take care of. I obviously put in the helipad and we remodeled the command center. It was just a dumpy old little modular trailer that was complete shambles. We've remodeled that to what you see on television. That's what I've been doing the last four years.

