<v SPEAKER_1>That's amazing. There must have been as much hard work as that sounds. Getting to create that little command center is like a tech geek's dream. When that door opened for the first time and you see all those screens, it's like I paused the TV that I was watching. I was just looking around going, oh my God, how many TVs? They've got at least 9, 10, 11, 12 cameras on display just now and then I've got so many TVs here and there's just boxes everywhere and you would just love five, 10 minutes in that room to go and just mess about with it to see what you can do. So that must have been a lot of fun. Can I just ask then, so that takes us up to the almost present day, the last year or so, when did you first start to hear that there's potentially going to be a show made about the ranch? Was that quite a quick process or was it something that was always in the offing?

00:33:39.608 --> 00:39:14.668
<v SPEAKER_2>Now, so it's something that once we determined that there was something happening on the ranch and that discussion turned, okay, yeah, we're seeing things happen here. Now, what do we do with this information? Do we keep it to ourselves like Bigelow did? Do we share out in a public forum? If we share it in a public forum, how do you do that? Is that just something like we make a YouTube video and put it up? Is this something you put in the Science Journal? It really became how do you release this information? About the same time, and I want to make sure I get my dates here, but the entire time we owned the ranch, one of the first things we had to do, there was a road that went right through the middle of the ranch that was a public road. When we bought it, the county notified Brandon that we couldn't lock the gate anymore. There was a public road, we had to let the public go across. Which put a lot of panic through the team, because it's like, how can you carry on an ongoing science investigation if you've got tourists going through the middle of your laboratory? And so I was able to go to the county and get that road vacated and shut down. And when I did that, it required public hearings. And Brandon, he didn't want this to stigmatize his business. You know, this whole paranormal world doesn't always mix well when you're talking about business professionals and real estate, and you're trying to sell a building to people and they think you're a quack, you know, because you're chasing ghosts and goblins. So Brandon was very adamant that people not know his identity. So anytime we had to go public with something, we always did it through my name. So I was the one that attended the public hearings. I was the one that got the road vacated. We did, Brandon let me, he reached out and said, hey, release a few of these photos on the Internet. So I would put a few photos out. So when people went searching for Skinwalker Ranch, my name was the only one that they could find associated with it. And because of that, I had both the blessing and the curse of having hundreds of people reach out to me on a regular basis, asking, can we come on the ranch or we want this or we want that? And part of that was, is at least once or twice a month, I got solicited from some type of movie producer or TV producer that was wanting to do a show on Skinwalker Ranch. And I'd always just take most of them. Brandon kind of charged me with the task of vetting through them and picking out which ones are serious, not just TV producers, but anybody that contacted us in regards to the ranch. And so Brandon would have me vet them and I'd have these TV people reach out and I would look and be, no, and so 99% of them got filtered out. And I had one guy about the same time that now the discussion is, we're starting to have this discussion of, well, how do we release this to the public? I had a TV producer named TJ. Allard. He's one of the executive producers on the show. And TJ reached out to me and introduced himself, and eventually convinced me into taking a phone call. And he pitched this idea of allowing TV cameras to come on and document our process, our science investigation. And so, I told him, we're not interested. You're always welcome to continue to call back. That's fine. You're a nice guy. I'll take your call. Well, TJ then started to reach out to me every single month, and he did so for almost a year. Hey, man, just checking in. You think it's something you guys would be interested in? Well, about month six or seven, eight of this, I don't know the exact time, but I did reach out to Brandon and I said, you know, we're having this discussion on how to release this. I've got this TV guy that's pitched this idea, and he's been pretty persistent. Brandon at the time was like, I don't know if we really want to do that. I was like, no problem. Then as time went on, TJ kept being persistent. Finally, I said, all right, I'll let you talk to Jim Morse. Jim's the manager. If Jim thinks it's a good idea, then we'll take you to Brandon. Jim talked to him for another couple of months. Then finally, Jim's like, yeah, T.J.'s the real deal. He's a good guy. It's worth having a conversation. I think TJ may have even flown in to talk to Brandon. I'm not sure about that. But TJ finally got to talk to Brandon, and thus the idea of the whole network series came in to being, and that's kind of how it came to be. It's not something that we went out looking for. I have to give TJ a lot of credit for being persistent and following up every single month, and he just didn't give up. It took a long time from when we first contacted, and then even after that point that he had that conversation with Brandon, and I think it was a long time after that, before it became a reality, so.

00:39:16.068 --> 00:39:25.088
<v SPEAKER_1>So on The Ranch at this point, we're getting to the point now where we're about to start filming the show. Is it yourself, Jim Morse? Is Jim Sagala there as well?

00:39:26.988 --> 00:40:46.968
<v SPEAKER_2>So our ranch team from the beginning has basically been myself and Eric Bard, the principal investigator, Jim Morse, who's the ranch manager, and Bryant Arnold, Dragon. He's been there from the beginning. And then Jim Sagala, he has an interesting past in the fact that he has done a lot of work that is tied in with the ranch through other individuals. So he's tied in with some of the scientists that worked for Mr. Bigelow, Mr. Bigelow's investigation. And so Jim Sagala had a lot of knowledge as far as the Bigelow days. And then with my injury, that's kind of Jim Sagala, he was one of the three individuals that investigated my head injury. And so it made sense to bring him in because one, he's coming at it from kind of that biomedical side with my injury. And then he also had pretty good knowledge of some of the things that Bigelow had done during Bigelow days. So he's been tied into the ranch for quite some time, but not necessarily like on our branch team, if that makes sense.

00:40:47.368 --> 00:41:08.488
<v SPEAKER_1>Yeah, of course, that's really interesting to know. How did things start to change on the day-to-day running of the ranch as the cameras and crew? And I'm guessing to you, Janie's people start to come in for you guys, because you've now had it, what, two to three years at this point working on the ranch. And I'm guessing things changed to some degree. What was that like?

00:41:09.068 --> 00:45:36.528
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. So they absolutely did change. I mean, you know, well, Jim Sagala had been on the ranch several times before, and he'd been working behind the scenes on my investigation, on my head injury, and been working on some of the science as well. Travis was new to the scene. You know, we're bringing on a new skeptical scientist. And when you bring a new scientist on, it almost is like you have to go back to the beginning and say, we've done this, this, this, this. And then they want to, of course, see that for themselves. They want to be able to verify for themselves what's going on. And then the investigation, so it is authentic on TV as far as we're not dramatizing, it's not scripted. They left it up to us to do what we wanted to do. I was never told what to say as far as like, you know, do this or do that. So it's authentic that way, but it did change the fact that there's a lot of the science that would just put people to sleep. It's definitely not made for TV. And so for those couple of months that you have the cameras there, you're really focused more on the visual side of things. We've been trying to process the data from the summer filming since the cameras turned off and went away because we gathered a lot of data and then you're trying to go through and post-process it, figure out, plot it, figure out what it all means. So it changes in the format that we're trying to share what our investigation is with the audience, which is legit, but at the same time, even things like when we're measuring RF, for example, we're out there. Well, we're all wearing microphones. The cameras are all broadcasting wirelessly. We're creating a lot of radio frequencies just by our presence being there. There's nothing abnormal about that. So when you have a meter and it's spiking and it's showing high levels of RF, well, a lot of that could just be caused by our cameras and our microphones and our self-emitted, all our cell phones and all the equipment that's there. The process becomes a little harder because you have to eliminate the known sources from the unknown sources, which means if you're starting to see those spikes, you got to turn off the cameras, you got to turn off the microphones, you got to turn off the cell phones. You've got to eliminate everything you possibly can that might be emitting that signal so that you can tell that that signal is legit and not just manmade. So there's a lot of that stuff that you have to learn how to work with the film crew and try to figure out. We're out there with, I think it's episode 3 when we're out there trying to triangulate, and there's a camera shot of us like we got these coffee cans. It's Jim Sagala and Travis and I. We got these coffee cans with these little RF meters, right? And I've taken a lot of criticism for this as I'm sure Travis and Jim, but we're like holding this RF meter out in front of the coffee can, which obviously defeats the whole purpose. And so people are like, you're not even using those right. You guys don't know what the heck you're doing. Well, when the meter is inside the coffee can and you've got to hold it so that you can still see inside the can and see the meter, but keep it inside. The camera can't see that. It doesn't make for a good camera shot for the audience. So it's balancing. It's like actually doing the exercise and then being able to film it in a way that the audience could get an idea of what it is that we're trying to accomplish. And so there's so much of that, that's like, oh, we need to, you got to pull that out of the can where we can pick that up with the camera. And so that's a lot of the adjustments. Trying to learn how to do the science and make it work for the camera guys so that we can give them their sexy shot that's going to bring ratings to the television show.

00:45:36.568 --> 00:46:47.168
<v SPEAKER_1>This is something I've addressed with Dan when we do the Watch Along show. And I love the show and I can compare it to the unidentified series with Tom DeLong and Luisa Elizondo. I don't know if you watched any of that around obviously the Tic Tac UFO. And it's again History Channel produced it. It's, you know, that's about UFOs and the US government's recent admissions. And then this is here's a serious scientific investigation on history again about Skinwalker Ranch. So the comparisons are probably pretty fair. So something then I'll address now. So the reception of season one, there was a lot of great reviews. The ratings were really good. I've actually been involved in a conversation with a few people today where Brandon has been destroying some skeptics fantastically on Twitter, which I love to watch him do because he's very eloquent and he's extremely intelligent and I love the way he comes across. However, like you've said yourself, there's a lot of mixed feelings on the production, potentially manufactured, forced incidents, and you've addressed that there when you say that you've got to do certain things for different camera shots. Have you watched the full season back? Would you say any of those criticisms were fair around the production aspect of it?

00:46:47.528 --> 00:49:03.648
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. I know what we filmed and then just like the regular viewers, I'm sitting down on television and you have to realize that we filmed all summer and that's condensed down to eight. I don't know what they are, 45 or 50-minute episodes. You're taking weeks and weeks and weeks of filming and editing it down for 45 minutes episode. I think the frustrating thing with the whole production is, first of all, I think that Prometheus did a wonderful job. I want to be clear that I think they did a great job of trying to balance, showing the viewer the science that's going on, but putting it in a format that's going to be watchable for 95 percent of the people that are not. Those of us that are in the science, we want the details. We could do without the drama, without the buildups and that. We want to know what are the facts here? What's the data? What are you seeing? That's what's important. But you have to understand that 95 percent of the viewers are coming home from work. They're kicking their feet up. They just want something to entertain them for an hour, not think about their day. Prometheus has had the challenge of trying to edit that and keep it true to the science, but at the same time, I'm just going to say it, dramatizing it in ways. I have to say as I'm watching it, like if I'm being 100% honest, I'm happy with most of, I'm happy with probably 90% of everything they did. I'm happy with, I understand they had to portray it somehow, and I'm happy with the way they did it. There's 10% of it probably that I cringe, and I say, I wish that they would have shown that differently because that should have, in my opinion, that should have been portrayed differently, or they shouldn't, you know, Episode 6 was hard for me. I did not like the way that that was edited. I'm just going to say it. I didn't like the way that Episode 6 was edited.

00:49:04.748 --> 00:49:09.888
<v SPEAKER_1>Just for the listeners that might not be sure of Episode 6, what was the particular incident you weren't happy about?

00:49:12.108 --> 00:51:23.708
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, that's the episode with the drilling and then the alpacas, and specifically dealing with the whole alpaca incident of them being attacked. I was very unhappy about the way that that was portrayed and edited. I felt like in some ways it was deceiving as to what the way that it was filmed. Let's just put it that way. I have to be careful because I'm not here to throw anybody under the bus, but I do feel like I have the right to have a voice and say, I'm happy with the way that was shown and the way that that was not shown. But for me, yes, there are parts of it that I'm like, I wish that would have been portrayed differently. But overall, like I say, 90 percent of it, I'm happy with what they showed, and maybe that's also because I was there. So when I see the criticisms and the critics, oh, you're using the coffee cans wrong. Yeah, it does show us using those wrong, but we did do it the right way. The cameras just didn't, they chose not to use that film because it didn't look as good as this stuff. So in my mind, I can be at peace with that because I think the critics have everything. He's right. We were using those cans wrong. So I can't blame him for filling that way. But it was just a representation. We did use them the correct way. We did go through and, you know, the camera. Like, for example, when we have to take the cameras down to check for RF, that's something that's not getting filmed. So the audience can't see us doing our very best to be true to science and trying to eliminate, okay, was it this? Was it that? And so yeah, it's one of those things where it's almost like a marriage, you know, you got to compromise a little bit and, you know, you're not going to get perfect. So you just strive to get for something that you can both live with. And that's kind of what I feel like with this TV series.

00:51:24.428 --> 00:53:07.228
<v SPEAKER_1>No, I really appreciate the honesty on that. And I think when you get involved in something like the community, whether it's paranormal, the UFO community, you know, people who see a spiritual side to what's going on or whatever it is, they're very protective because this is something for their whole life. It's been theirs. There'll be people that for Skinwalker Ranch, this is what it means to them. And when people try and make it look like something it's not, they get protective. And the UFO community is one of the worst for that. It's great, but it's also brutal at times as well, the slightest thing. So I get that. And you're right that it's entertainment at the end of the day. And if you want to move the conversation forward and you want to bring a new audience to kind of see what's being done there with the investigation, and does that involve eventually getting more funding, more eyes on the project, and moving it into a bigger discussion, then you do have to aim at people in an entertaining way. Because like you say, as incredible as the science is, and the data is going to be the thing that ultimately gives us answers, it's not going to get ratings. And ratings, if they're low, aren't going to renew shows. So there's a balance there to be found. And I think if I had anything, and this is just my own personal for season two, whoever's in charge of mixing the music and the dramatic headshots of, you know, when you look to the side, and I just get that cringe of, ah, he shouldn't have a look. But I reckon the look, and particularly Dragon, some of his looks, I think, has he actually done that at that time? Or have they taken a totally random shot from five minutes before, where someone's been ordered in a Chinese and he's not been happy with what's came, what's been delivered, and they've just used that as the shot. So I always worry about that on these reality-based series. But yeah, that's an absolute correlation there. Both.

00:53:08.048 --> 00:53:13.488
<v SPEAKER_2>Sometimes they're real, and sometimes they were edited from five minutes earlier when the Chinese was not right.

00:53:13.488 --> 00:53:45.868
<v SPEAKER_1>So yes. Do you know what? I can live with that though. You get that. It's getting an audience, and do you know what? It has been renewed for a second season. I'm going to wrap up on talking about season two. But just to go back, then, you've mentioned a few times about your head and your injury. So what we see is that before the season started a couple of years previous, you had done some digging. I believe the shot used in the show was with an excavator or some kind of digging and equipment. And off the back of that, I mean, your words will be better than mine. Do you want to just quickly talk through what happened and what we can see on the show?

00:53:47.468 --> 01:03:48.428
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah. So yeah, I was using a skid steer. And I was actually carving into the side of the mesa. There was a section of ranch up on the mesa that security could only access by foot. We'd had some issues with trespassers. And by the time you get up on top of mesa with the foot, they're gone and we've dealt with vandalism, all kinds of stuff. So my fix for that was, hey, I'll carve a road up on top so we can get up on there with the UTVs and then they can't outrun us. We can better patrol the perimeter of the ranch. I started to do this excavation and it was during the course of doing it. Matter of fact, so that particular, that particular road that I was working on lies unfinished today. It's about three quarters of the way finished. Still doesn't get up on top. But the one thing that I fought with the network on a little bit was they kept saying, matter of fact, I saw a rough cut of the first episode and they had me and I was digging and then my head started to swell up. And I told them, if you put that in there, I will sing like a canary because that's not what happened. I was not digging and it started to swell up. See, the fact of the matter is, while there have been literally dozens of incidents where I have been digging and then something bad happens, correlation does not mean that you can't point for sure and say, oh, that happened because you dug. It could have seriously been a series of coincidences that have nothing to do with each other. But it happens enough that it does give us pause and it's like, okay, it's something we need to keep in the back of mind, we need to be careful of. In this particular case, here I am defacing the mesa to put this road in, just this little UTV trail up the side. My injury started on a Saturday. I hadn't even been to the ranch in a couple of days. It had been two days since I've been at the ranch, or three days. I don't remember now exactly, but on the back of my head, on the lower part of my head, on Saturday night, it was actually my birthday. I can remember the date perfect because it started on my birthday, March 4th. That night when I went to bed, I noticed that I had a sore spot on the back of my head. It was like a bruise, about a little bit, probably like one inch in diameter. I didn't think much of it. We'd actually spent my birthday at a funeral of a loved one. So I'd been sat all day inside of a church house at a funeral, hadn't done any physical activity. Went to bed when I woke up Sunday morning, that bruise had swollen up. It was really tight. It was like a little goose egg. It was extremely painful. My thought was, maybe I'd just gotten a spider bite, or some type of insect had bitten me, and I had reacted to it. I mentioned it to my wife. Sunday morning, we went to church, and as church was going on, as this hour rolled on, I started to feel sicker and sicker. I finally told my wife that I was going to leave church early. I went home, laid down, and my head just hurt really, really bad. I felt nauseous. I thought maybe I had the flu. At this point, I was pretty certain that I'd gotten an insect bite and it was making me sick. I'm otherwise an extremely healthy person. I've had physicals. I passed them with flying colors. I'm a healthy individual. Every test that I've taken, my markers have always come back excellent. I wasn't worried. I never go to the doctor unless I absolutely am dying and I have to. My wife came home. She was concerned about me. Sunday progressed into Sunday evening and then into Monday morning. I was now pretty sick. I was not up on my feet. And Monday, she wanted to take me to the hospital and I refused to let her. I told her, no, I'll be fine. It's just a spider bite. Like, let it run its course. I'll start feeling better soon. And then by Monday night, I was in extreme pain. And starting Monday night, probably around 10 p.m., the goose egg went, it stayed pretty much the same size all of Sunday and all of Monday. But coming to Monday night, the goose egg started to grow and it started to spread. And what ended up happening was that my, the fatty tissue of my scalp separated from my skull. And so I basically got scalped. And Monday night into Tuesday morning, it's the most excruciating pain I've ever felt in my life. I was, I can't even describe the amount of pain I was in. And I just toughed it out. At this point, I'm not, I don't know that I was thinking 100% straight, but my wife woke up Tuesday morning, and she saw that my head had completely swollen up. There was a definite line on my forehead where it had separated, and it continued to slowly move down my forehead towards my eyes. And so she freaked out, loaded me up, took me to the hospital where I was admitted, and ended up spending the week in the hospital. And by the end of the third day, I could pretty much grab the hair on my head and walk my face around my skull because it wasn't attached. It was detached. It was pretty gruesome. And because of my association with the ranch, I was only out of the hospital a day or two when I was contacted by Brandon and the team and asking me if I'd be willing to allow some people to come investigate it. And so I had only been home a couple of days when the investigation into what had taken place. Now, mind you, when I was in the hospital, my doctors could not figure out what was going on. I did over, I think they ran 43 different tests. They were able to rule out insect bites. They were able to rule out. Every test I took, my doctor would come back into my room after the test, after the lab results came back and he'd shake his head and say, you're the healthiest sick person I've ever met. All my lab tests were coming back, not just good, but they were spot on perfect. When they biopsied the fluid in my head, I mean, it came back 100 percent sterile. They couldn't even, I mean, it was nothing. Everything that they were seeing in my results justified what they should be seeing. My doctor used the term medically impossible several times with me. They could not figure out what was going on. When they released me from the hospital, I even had to sign a paper on the release forms that was the hospital saying, we have no idea what caused this. We have no idea what happened to you. Therefore, we don't know what to tell you to stay away from and maybe sign that paper. Then the investigation site started. These individuals had three individuals that were investigating my injury very closely. It's been over the course of the past two or three years. They've had me continue to take further tests and more MRIs, more all kinds of different tests. It's through this investigation process that as time goes on and as they learn more about what took place and have dove into my medical records and everything, that the confidence level has increased dramatically, that it is associated with the ranch and that it did occur on the ranch, but in all honesty, none of us can say 100% sure that it happened on the ranch or that it, you know. But the investigation and where we're being led shows that, yes, it is tied into the ranch. And that's why I think the network continues to and they bring it up all the time. I think if I cringe a lot of us, because like, oh, seriously, you're showing that picture again. But would that be the picture of the scan, where it shows the fluid and both the scan and then the one that I'm in the hospital, where I look like total, I look like, oh man, they show the picture of me in the hospital, my head bandaged. And I've seen that. I don't know how many times I've seen that in the episodes. And I'm like, seriously, you're showing that picture again. So but that's one of the reasons why they do is because the investigation, which to be honest with the viewers, there are a lot of facts with my injury that are not public. And I don't know if they ever will become public. Some of that's not really in my hands because of the things that they've unearthed. So it does appear like it is associated with the ranch and the investigation. Careful, careful, long, disciplined investigation has shown that indications are strong, that it is ranch related. And then comes the speculation as to what caused it, why.

01:03:49.268 --> 01:04:49.808
<v SPEAKER_1>It's good to hear you say that even though it's something you've experienced and you know, like you say, the pain you went through and you've had this separate investigation, which I'm sure people are going to love to hear the kind of mysterious side of it. But for you, it's trying to get some answers. And if that's something that's getting you closer to that, that's really good to hear. And from our point of view, it's great you're fit and healthy. But then there's also the potential that this could be a warning shot from whatever the phenomena is on the ranch. Because like you say, yeah, you've done some digging, you've defaced it, you've changed it. And if that's something that we're not observant, it's fine. But actually, like we know, the first episode is called, Bad Things Happen When You Dig, for a reason. You know, something bad happened when you dug. It does flare up again near the start of the season. And everyone seems really concerned. I know you take yourself away from the scene, don't you? How much did that shape the safety protocols going forward? And did that have an impact on the show going forward? That, you know, you'd started to feel that kind of pain again.

01:04:51.408 --> 01:10:07.848
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, so I've had several small reoccurrences and all ranch related. And each time you think you've got it behind you, you think that it's in your past. When that happened, when we were out there, now it's interesting, here we are shining lasers up on the mesa. And, and then next thing I know, like my head is in that, you know, the concerning thing was it wasn't like the pain was excruciating. I mean, it hurts. I'm not going to lie. The pain hurt. It was not the same kind of pain that I felt with my first injury. But it was it was the fact that it was taking place in that exact same spot that it started the first time. That was a concerning thing. It's like, great. Here we go again. It did start to swell up again. And this time, if the producers want me to go out there and say, we were shining lights on the mesa and my head started to swell up, I'll do it because we were out there shining lights on the mesa and my head started to swell up. I was on the ranch and this starts to happen again. And it did shake us. And there was a whole discussion that went on in my absence. And then also, I was taking, I took some phone calls off site too while this whole thing is going on. And Brandon is not about to let his curiosity be more important than the health of his team. Brandon is very, very people-oriented. And his concern is genuine. It's not dramatized. It's not contrived. And so there was discussion. Brandon was seriously saying, I don't know if this is worth it, guys. Like, he had a conversation with me. He's like, Tom, like, is it really worth it? Are we playing with fire? Should we pull out? You know, I don't want, this isn't worth somebody that I care about deeply getting hurt. And, and then, you know, you see, you see some of that discussion on TV. I think it's an episode three when I come back. But it is something that I can't, in my heart, I can't believe that all this stuff that's happening on the ranch is like honoring the little barbed wire fence that goes around the border, that somehow that fence is keeping all the ghost and goblins in, right? And so there's a good chance that a lot of the things that we're seeing on the ranch are happening in the surrounding area as well, which we have a lot of people. Even though we're rural, you know, you have hundreds if not thousands of people within a short proximate distance that, um, that call this home. And, uh, and as long as we've got somebody like Brandon that has the money and the resources to come in and investigate it, um, I, I had a little bit of fear in my heart, like, don't pull out because, you know, yeah, Bigelow came and investigated and what did we get out of it? We don't know what we got. Nothing came of it. They locked everything behind. So as long as we've got someone like Brandon that's willing to foot the bill and pay for this investigation, I don't want to lose that opportunity. And we owe it to the surrounding areas to see if this is something that's just on the ranch or maybe there's something dangerous in the surrounding areas as well. And so, um, we, we pushed on and I encouraged him not to, not to run, but it shaped the rest of the season. I think what it did is it made Travis because Travis thought we were all a bunch of scaredy cats. And matter of fact, his exact words to us before the TV series even started is he thought we were all batshit crazy. And I think that my injury kind of snapped him out of it and brought, made it real for him that this is a, this is not something we're just goofing around about. We're not just saying these things to make it sound mysterious. Like there are real effects going on here. I think if anything, my injury made Travis realize that there are consequences and that the investigation needs to be done, but it needs to be done safely. It needs to be done cautiously. I know that's kind of when his view of Dragon changed because at that point he realized that we have good reason to be cautious about what we're doing. And there's a reason why we haven't just been running around poking the hornet's nest. So it did change the rest of the season. It'll change the rest of the investigation as far as the show goes, because I think that's what made it real like, oh man, somebody like, okay, people could get her. And then you see later on in the later episodes that Travis himself starts to experience some of these same things. And so it validated the fact that we need to be cautious.

01:10:09.048 --> 01:10:27.088
<v SPEAKER_1>Absolutely. And that incident was highlighted, you know, again, hopefully something doesn't happen again to you, because it can't be a nice thing to experience. However, was there anything that the cameras did or didn't pick up that you would put alongside that as some of the strangest things that you've been a part of or witnessed while you've been on the ranch?

01:10:29.068 --> 01:14:51.448
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, so my injury is definitely the top. And then because I spend so much time on the ranch, as far as my physical, like physically present on the ranch, I spend just as much, if not more time than anybody else on the ranch team and have done since day one. I have had the opportunity, blessing or curse, I'm not sure, of having a lot of experiences on the ranch. I've experienced tear. And this is a feeling where I didn't see anything. I couldn't, like I couldn't tangibly see what the danger was. But there was like a neurological or psychological effect. I think, I think it had to do some type of, we've recorded these high electromagnetic. My brain is shortening out now. We've registered a lot of these radio frequencies that are extremely high, borderline dangerous. And there have been times on this ranch and they've happened at night. I've been over there doing my responsibilities. One night was when I was plowing snow. And I actually heard an audible voice associated with this. And it told me to stop and don't go any further. And when I ignored this voice, I know for a fact it was not audible. Like it happened inside my head. But you couldn't have told me the difference. It sounded just as if you're talking to me. But I know it wasn't audible because I was plowing snow. My skid steer was making so much noise. And then I had earbuds in my ears listening to an audio book. So somebody could have been standing three feet away from me, yelling at me. And I may not have heard them because of all the noise the machine was making. But I heard the voice crystal clear, which tells me it came from inside my head, not outside my head. I ignored the warning. It told me to not go any further. And immediately, when I continued down on... When I came, I was plowing snow. So I went back to the ranch house and I was coming back. And when I got to that point that I'd been warned not to go any further, I just thought, well, that's stupid. I'm just imagining this stuff. And I kept going and I only made it maybe 100 feet. And all of a sudden this terror gripped me, this fear. And it's unlike anything I've ever experienced in my entire life. I've been scared before, but nothing like this. And it became so bad that I was shaking. Like I was like at Parkinson's, I was shaking so severely I couldn't control it. And these times when this fear comes over me, the psychological or the neurological effects, I had one, well, this particular time I shook for about 12 hours before I stopped shaking. I couldn't control it. It was outside of my ability to control it. The next time it was almost 24 hours before I stopped shaking. So I've had that happen. I've seen things like, there was one day I thought I saw a dog walking across the road. We'd had some animals, some pack of dogs chasing the livestock. And so I saw this dog crossing the road and I thought I would go up and catch it. And when I got up to it, it wasn't there, like it just vaporized. So it was like a mirage type thing. We've had the equipment. I've had my cell phone, you see on the episode where the cell phones start going crazy. I've had my phone gripped. I've seen batteries go from 100% to zero in under two minutes. I've had, I'm trying to think, I've had a lot of things with my equipment take place. So many that I could literally spend two hours just telling you about the experience, maybe four hours talking about the experiences I've seen. But I've seen shadow figures over in the homesteads. So all kinds of strange, crazy things.

01:14:52.308 --> 01:15:35.988
<v SPEAKER_1>That's the beauty of the ranch, isn't it? And the area that there's, it's not just one phenomena, isn't just there's UFOs, it's not just there's, there's lights on the mesa or, you know, it's not you're getting these weird sounds coming from underneath the homes. There's a lot going on, you know, huge dire wolf type creatures, portals potentially opening up left, right and center. So that's the kind of mystique and the nature of the ranch. Obviously, we've got a second series, Brandon himself has said should be filming soon. If you've got anything more like, do you know any more about the dates or what we can kind of expect from a second season and even if you're kind of bound by networks that you can't discuss that kind of detail, what would you like to see in a second season?

01:15:37.688 --> 01:21:04.348
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, yeah, so Brandon being the boss, can get away with a lot of things I can't. So I took his lead. He tweeted out the other day that we were having a second season and I took the lead and was like, sweet, it's public knowledge now, I can share it with people and then only to be told that, no, I can't share that with people yet and it hasn't been made official. So I'll let Brandon take the lead on that stuff. He seems to be able to get away with it. That's fair. But I will say, and I've been vocal about this with Brandon and I've been vocal about it with the network and our meetings, when you're looking forward to what would... Hypothetically, if there was a second season, what would we do? We discovered a lot of things in episode one, I mean, in season one that I'd like to go back and revisit and more meticulously and carefully investigate out. There was a lot of loose ends left hanging that need followed up on. In addition, there's things in second season that I hope... I told the network that I sincerely hope in second season that they won't be less... In season one, they did not... They have this rule, they called it the fourth curtain, and that is that they don't want to become the story. They're there to capture the story. They don't want to become the story. So the production crew experienced a lot of things themselves. Their equipment was not immune to the anomalies that take place on the ranch. They struggled with batteries going dead. They struggled with getting equipment to work properly. Matter of fact, there's a huge section of filming that we did that got wiped mysteriously on a section. They experienced strange things on that ranch as well. Unfortunately, I feel like when you don't document those, then it leads to the criticism and justly so that says, well, why is it that that guy's $35,000 drone won't work, but the network's drone works just fine to capture it. It must be made up. It must be, this is just fake. I really hope that season 2, that when, not if, but when they experienced some of the same troubles that we're experiencing, the audience can see that and be shown behind the curtain and be like, oh, the network's cameras just went down and we don't know why, we can't figure it out rather than that never being shown. So I hope that that is shown. I hope that they allow us to show more mundane things. And by that, I mean technically every object in the sky is a UFO for at least a minute until your brain registers, that's a plane or that's a helicopter or that's a drone. We see so many UFOs on the ranch, it's unbelievable. But they might be a UFO for a day, they might be a UFO for a week, they might be a UFO for a year until we finally are able to crack the mystery of what that was. That was flight 1248 United going to London and for whatever reason, it didn't show up or whatever it is. And truth be told, 98 or 99% of the UFOs we see on the ranch, yeah, they're UFOs for a time. But then as we do the investigation, as Eric does his post-processing on the video and all that, at some point, we learn that it's just of mundane origin. And so I hope that they allow us to show some of those things so that people can see. We do our due diligence and we're not just drinking Kool-Aid. Not every fly on the camera lens is a UFO. Not every snowflake out there is an orb. But if that's all you show on television and that's all the audience sees, then how are they supposed to know that you've eliminated? You're calling this an unknown object because you've already eliminated out the insects and the bugs and the planes and the drones and the helicopters and the dust and all the things that can look damn impressive in a camera. But actually at the end of the day, it's just regular. If they'll allow us to show the guys going through that process of eliminating it and finding out that, oh, that was really cool to see. But at the end of the day, that was actually a piece of dust that was reflecting the infrared camera lens. So I think that will help bring credibility to the show if the audience is able to see behind the curtain just a little bit more and see, and I don't want to call them failures because it's not a failure, but see some of the debunking that takes place.

01:21:05.068 --> 01:22:06.948
<v SPEAKER_1>No, that would be great. You would think it especially in 2020 or more than likely 2021 when we potentially get to see a second season, if they make a second season, of course. You've got a lot of different platforms where you could put content like that, but if they don't want it in a main show that they still want to make dramatized or really focus on entertainment aspect, and you talked about some of the bold and science or the more mundane things, you could put that on a YouTube or you could put that on Twitter or whatever kind of platform they want. If they want to put on their own website, here's bonus content for people. Yeah, I'm sure there would be a market for that because that's something that definitely when I go through the Twitter feeds and the message boards, people question these things. The interest is definitely there. That probably takes us nicely, Thomas, on to some listener questions because the first one is linked on to what you've just said there. Ashley had a few questions for you. What kind of experiments would Thomas and the team like to do next? Also, are there any experiments you've done, you think you could have improved on?

01:22:09.248 --> 01:24:16.988
<v SPEAKER_2>I want better GPR. If Thomas gets to call the shots and I get to do this then, I want better, not, I'm not saying the guys that did in season 1 didn't do a good job. I'm saying they weren't able to do enough of the job. I want to go back, revisit that. And I want to investigate further over at the Homestead 2. You know, where we did the drilling. I think that we need to look into that more. You know, it's funny, I actually made a whole list the other day because I did submit a list saying, girl, you know, if there were a season 2, these are some of the ideas that I would like to explore further, if I had to say. But we triangulated, I think, one of the biggest things is we keep picking up these strange signals, these strange RFs. And when we've done the rockets, when we did the weather balloon, the data seems to suggest that the higher up we're going, the stronger the signals are getting, which would be opposite of what should be happening, according to the scientists. So, I would really like to drill down and go higher, figure out a way to put equipment up higher for longer, and get some good data that's not just a few seconds long, like the, you know, the rockets were great, but you're really limited in time as to the sample size that you're able to get. So, I'd like to really look further at the RF signals that we're picking up out there. And then, I'd like to branch out a little bit, and I would like to see us setting up some instrumentation that would allow us to tell, you know, when we're seeing something on the ranch, are we seeing it other places in the Una Basin as well? So, I'd like to branch out a little bit and see, kind of take a wider area and see if we can get a, an idea of just how localized this is to the ranch.

01:24:17.868 --> 01:25:12.948
<v SPEAKER_1>Awesome. I suppose the ideal was, you know, when you done that rocket experiment, what we were all going to hopefully see was the rocket shooting into the sky and then about, you know, a couple of hundred feet up, hitting something, dropping out the sky, and it being like, oh, there's something sitting there. It's got an invisibility cloak or it's hidden, and we just can't see it. But, but yeah, so, but no, but some of those experiments, like you say, they would intrigue you enough, you want to see more and you kind of want to take it that next step further. And again, that ties in with what you said earlier, that these experiments for all, they've got a monetary value, like someone like Brandon. Okay, so if there's something in the first one you do, let's spend a bit more next time in investigating it further. And again, that would be great for season two. So thanks for that question, Ashley. Steven had a question or two questions. Are you aware of the history of different UFO cases? And if so, do you see any patterns with what goes on around the world and what goes on in the ranch in terms of UFOs?

01:25:14.788 --> 01:26:24.828
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, so before I became associated with the ranch, this is not a area that caught my interest. I did not spend any time on UFO theories or in the paranormal world. As a businessman, like I said, I didn't even believe in this stuff. Since becoming associated with the ranch, as more and more things have happened on the ranch, then obviously it's led me to reach out and start looking at, you know, do they see these things in other places? I think the Tic Tacs is a very pretty visible one that's come to light recently with the Pentagon releasing the Tic Tac videos and finally admitting that, yes, we're encountering these things. We're not sure what they are. And so in doing that, I've studied a little bit. I'm far from calling myself an expert on any of it. I have, I know just enough to make me dangerous and but I am, I am learning more and I am studying more about it and trying to get myself better versed in what people are seeing in other places. And so then it can help me better identify what we're seeing on the ranch.

01:26:25.428 --> 01:26:38.388
<v SPEAKER_1>Awesome. Stephen's follow up question, I suppose is really going to push for how much you want to answer this. And it's absolutely no doubt going to be more of a guess or assessment on your part. What do you think is buried under the ranch?

01:26:44.348 --> 01:28:37.808
<v SPEAKER_2>All I will say with that is I'm not sure. But the longer that I'm there and the more, the more that I see, the more open-minded I am to the possibility that there is indeed something below the ranch. And I can't help but think that if there is something under there, maybe it's of ancient origin, some type of piece of advanced technology, some type of AI. What we deal with on the ranch, it seems to have an intelligence. I will tell you, I could spend an hour here telling you of specific circumstances when we've had our equipment toyed with. I'm going to use the word toyed. Cameras turned on and off at precisely the exact same moment that we're picking up anomalies. It just so happens that it happens to be the camera that would have recorded that or the instrumentation that would have picked that up. We've seen this enough that the only way that my brain can come up with a logical explanation is that there is an intelligence to whatever it is at Skinwalker Ranch and that it is the cat, we are the mouse and we're being toyed with. So as far as what's being buried underneath, I'm very open-minded now. I would have laughed at that idea just even two years ago. Today, I would say I'm very open-minded to the fact that there possibly could be something buried under the ranch. If there is, I would say it's some type of source of intelligence, artificial or technology, I don't know, but that's my guess.

01:28:38.868 --> 01:29:15.428
<v SPEAKER_1>Awesome. I think Travis does a bit of a line-tracing, doesn't he, on the show? I don't know if this was more for dramatic effect, but definitely if you were going to draw a type of UFO, I'm sure in your top five shapes of alien spacecraft, that would have came out. But again, it is based on seismology, is that the right term, and sending signals under the ground and picking it back up. So yeah, that's pretty interesting. Can I just ask slightly on topic of what you've said, but this is a question from myself before I go back to listener questions. Has Brandon ever had any meetings with Lou Elizondo or Tom DeLong, or anyone at two of the stars that you know of?

01:29:16.428 --> 01:29:17.648
<v SPEAKER_2>Not that I know of, no.

01:29:18.188 --> 01:29:19.188
<v SPEAKER_1>Okay, cool.

01:29:20.708 --> 01:29:22.728
<v SPEAKER_2>He probably would have told me if he had.

01:29:23.348 --> 01:30:17.548
<v SPEAKER_1>Okay, cool. That would be good. That would be a great roundtable to have, I think, Brandon Fugal and Louise Elizondo, Tom DeLong, and a few others. That would be interesting to hear. Interesting and a question regarding the scene that, again, you talked about the alpacas, it drummed up a lot of controversy and you had your own issues with that. The scene in one of the last episodes with the death of the cattle or the cow, it's very much tied in on that episode. There's a UFO in the sky, the cattle's died, an expert veterinarian comes in, and he basically says there's been a massive stress that's came on, that stress-induced pneumonia, I believe is what he says has killed the cattle. But there's no getting away from whether it's the editing or what you guys wanted it to be. The inclination is that that UFO above the cattle had something to do with it dying. What were your thoughts on that scene and incident? Do you think it was a true reflection of what happened?

01:30:20.068 --> 01:35:24.048
<v SPEAKER_2>Yeah, so the official determination from the vet was that it was a rapid onset stress-induced pneumonia. He could tell from... So he was showing us when he was doing the neocropsy that if it was something that had built up over time, there's scar tissue that builds up in the lungs. And there was none of that. And so he said, it had to have happened very rapidly. And it was stress-induced pneumonia. So the cow got stressed. It happened really recently. And so that's... And you know what? Here's the thing. This is the tough thing about the ranch, is there's a million things that happen. So cows die every day on ranches. That's cows get pneumonia every day and they die. There's nothing strange about a cow getting pneumonia and dying. That's just part of ranching. But then on the other hand, you have to start saying, okay, that's the current train of thought. But what stressed the cow? What was it that made that cow get stressed enough that it caught pneumonia? Other stress-induced pneumonias of other ranchers, we say, oh, that happens all the time. Well, maybe we need to look a little closer and say, why does that happen all the time? Are there RF signals that their bodies are susceptible to that stressing them and causing that? Why are we seeing this? Sometimes things become so normal that we just accept that's just the way it is. That's just life. And maybe sometimes we need to question the, you know, question the answers, question the questions. And so because it is on the ranch, then that is naturally our question. Was this just a regular death and nothing strange about it? Or was there more to it? So when we start going back through the footage, and this is anytime we like with this cow, we're going to go back and look at all the data that we have available to us, leading up to this point, and see if we can figure out and understand what stressed that cow. If it's that a dog was out there chasing it, well, that explains it. There's nothing strange about that. So as we're looking at this footage, there's this object up in the sky. Now, the reason why that object, I think, merits further investigation is for two things. You could have a fly literally walking across the lens of your camera that could look identical to that. We've seen it a million times. With our cameras, it's really hard to gauge depth perception. How far away was that object? This particular object, at one point during the video, it appears, and Eric's still doing further post-processing, but it appears to us that the object disappears behind the tree branch and then comes out the other side. That's important because a fly would be in front of the tree branch, not behind it. Well, then that takes it to the next step, and if we can prove absolutely that that's what happened is it went behind the tree, that gives us an idea on distance. For the object to look that big in the camera and at least be the distance of the trees, we now can start taking measurements that tell us the size of that craft. And the size of that craft, if it's a fly flying one inch away from the screen, that explains why it looks like it's going 10,000 miles an hour, you know, in two frames it's gone. Well, it only went two inches, it was nothing. But if you're talking about a distance out there beyond the trees and it covered that amount of ground, now we could triangulate and we can see that that, for it to go that amount of ground, you know, it'd be going like a thousand miles an hour. I don't know of a drone or a helicopter that can go that fast. So that's kind of our thinking on this particular object. The fact that it is above the cow and, you know, in looking at it, there again, we can't be certain, but it does look like the cow's reactions while it's on the ground seem to be corresponding with the movement of this craft. So that is actually, it's not far from what we were seeing. And I'm sure that we got a few head movements and nods and some music in there to add to it. But it actually, when we went back, started looking at the footage, like that was, you know, I think that was edited pretty accurately.

01:35:24.988 --> 01:35:55.948
<v SPEAKER_1>Particularly if it's right, what you say about Brandon being the character he is in general, that he was particularly animated during the scene with London Malton Howe and Travis. And so yeah, no, that's a great answer. No, that's Brandon, that's Brandon. That sounds like that could be a catchphrase on the second season if Brandon's in it more. That's Brandon. So a couple more listener questions. I've got Dan asking, how are your injuries now? Anything, has anything happened regarding your head injury since season one wrapped up? And he also says he's glad that you're well.

01:35:56.568 --> 01:36:59.688
<v SPEAKER_2>Well, thank you for your concern. And I have not had any reoccurrences since the one that we saw in episode two. Thank goodness. And other than that, like I say, I'm in good health. I do have several, it's interesting, like my head is still very tender in that particular spot where it started. So my injury happened March 4th of 2017. And here it is 2020. And three years later, like my, the back of my head is still tender to the touch. And I still get weird sensations in my, inside my head and my scalp that I didn't have them before. I've only had them since my injury. So there are a few minor things, but overall I'm healthy. I feel good. Haven't had any more reoccurrences since that little one that we saw in episode two when we were out camping.

01:37:01.188 --> 01:37:20.648
<v SPEAKER_1>Awesome. It also asks, you've spoken the last episode and you've mentioned on the show as well, that strange events have happened at home and they persist to the present day. Do you care to share any particular stories or details of what seems to have followed you from the ranch to your own property? Yeah.

01:37:21.428 --> 01:44:34.868
<v SPEAKER_2>I will just say that since I started working in the ranch, we've had some not so scary things happen and we've had some outright terrifying things that have happened. My son just a couple of weeks ago, my oldest son, he's 15. My kids think it's cool that I'm on TV. There's a lot of perks that come with the ranch. We were able to go fly in Brandon's helicopter. There's some cool things like that that my kids really enjoy and think it's cool that dad's involved in this project. But that being said, my oldest son came to me just two or three weeks ago and just said, you still feel like it's worth it, dad. You see what's going on here at home and you feel like it's still worth it. It was a valid question. As far as specific instances, I'll refer him to my Twitter feed. If you go to my Twitter feed and I post it, a little video and an explanation about a time that happened with my car. The reason why I'm hesitant to share things that go on at my house is I shared this particular incident about my car and the windows rolling down. It's no different than the rest of the million UFO stories we've been told in the fact that in order for you to accept it, you have to overlook a lot of, for example, my windows rolled down and the specific camera of my surveillance system at my house, before the windows go down, it shuts itself off. None of the other seven cameras that are on the system work beautifully, never even so much as flickered. And yet the one on the front of my house facing this car goes off for a couple of minutes. There's no way people would have, for this to be significant, people have to take me at my word that I didn't go upstairs and turn that camera off, that I didn't just use the key fob and roll the windows down, that I didn't turn it off or walk outside. Like, there's a lot of holes here that I'm expecting people to believe. And the truth is, I don't expect them to believe. I'm simply just throwing an experience out there and saying, this is what's taken place. And as far as being able to prove it, well, I can't prove that my key fob was on my dresser the whole time, or my little nightstand. I can't prove that I didn't go upstairs and turn that camera off. And so because of that, I also can't prove that it happened the exact way it did. All I know is I came home from the ranch. It's a cold morning. It's 11 Fahrenheit below zero here in Roosevelt. My windows are rolled up. I pull in. I come in the house. My key fob, I only have one key fob to my car. I set it down. I was actually showering and getting ready. That's how I know for a fact. I didn't have the key fob in my pocket because I was showering and getting ready. My son comes and knocks on my bedroom door and says, Dad, your windows are all down in your car. The heck? Well, so I go outside and sure enough, the windows are all down. I try to roll them up. I can't roll them up. So I have to run in the house, grab my key fob, run out there, roll them up. I immediately go upstairs to my camera system to look and see how the heck my windows rolled themselves down. Lo and behold, that one camera facing my car went down for three minutes. Now I think it was a three-minute time period that it went down. Now, Eric helped me get into the log of my camera system, which shows every time it's been logged in to, it shows when it's been given a command to stop recording. It logs all of this stuff. There were no, nobody logged in to the system. It'd probably be a very easy system to hack. I'm not saying it's very secure, but nobody logged in to it. There were no commands given to that camera to shut off. It just decided to quit working for three minutes. The same three minutes that these windows rolled themselves down. My car does have, and I learned this from the viewers on Twitter. I appreciate it. I didn't know this, but my key fob, if I hold the unlock button long enough, the windows will roll themselves down. It's a feature that is built into the car. It is made to do that. I didn't know it was there when I put it on Twitter. I'm grateful for the Twitter followers that bring that to my attention. It explains part of the mystery, but I didn't have the key fob on me, so I know for a fact there was no way that key fob button got pushed and rolled those windows down. But then it brings it back. What is it that we see on the ranch all the time? It's the electromagnetic radiation that we're picking up, these radio frequencies. Well, that's all the key fob is. There's a radio frequency to the car, tells it to do that. And then the camera going down, again, another electronic. So, it's possible that whatever has the ability to hit that frequency and make that car do that. That's one example of things that we've seen. The list is long and I've had some things that have been serious enough that I've had to call the authorities, and some things that have terrified my family. So, there's those things that I know are real, and then I tell people, there's also the observer effect. I mean, you go buy a white jeep, and the next day you realize that 10 of your neighbors have the same white jeep, and you didn't notice that the day before, right? Like, we noticed those things. So, before I worked on the ranch, who's to say that I didn't have crazy things happening around me, but I just was oblivious to it. So, I try to take that into account and say, is it that there's really more crazy things going on at my house? Or is it simply that I work on the ranch and now I recognize and observe more crazy things? They were there the whole time, but I just didn't notice them. I, like, there's all these things you have to weigh out and balance and say, what is it? There are several of these serious things that have taken place that I know for a fact. Those things didn't happen before my association with the ranch. So, there's definitely some aspect or element of it. And then you add in the fact that so many people that have visited the ranch go home and whatever it is, this is their words, it followed them home. You know, the hitchhiker effect. I believe in it completely. I believe it's real, but that's just me.

01:44:36.128 --> 01:44:39.868
<v SPEAKER_1>No, awesome. Dan also asked, are you a cat or a dog person?

